<h2>Five things that are different about Electronic City homes</h2>
<h3>1. Hard water in the kitchen and bathrooms</h3>
<p>Many societies here rely on borewells and water tankers, and the water is hard. It leaves white rings on polished granite, clouds glass backsplashes and shower screens, and scales taps and sink baskets within months. Plan for it from the start: a steel 304 sink, sealed or quartz countertops, matte rather than gloss tiles behind the sink, and an electrical and water point for an RO purifier. Our <a href="/modular-kitchen/electronic-city-chandapura/">local modular kitchen guide</a> covers the details.</p>
<h3>2. Compact kitchens with a utility</h3>
<p>Most 2 BHK flats in the area have a kitchen of about 7×9 to 8×10 ft with a narrow utility beyond it. The utility takes the washing machine and the dishwasher's water, which frees the main kitchen for cooking. An L-shape works when the kitchen is squarish; a parallel layout works when it is long and narrow. See <a href="/modular-kitchen/l-shape-vs-u-shape/">L-shape vs U-shape</a> before you decide.</p>
<h3>3. Rental demand from the IT parks</h3>
<p>A large share of flats here are let to professionals working in the Electronic City campuses. If you are furnishing to rent, choose laminate over acrylic or PU, BWP plywood in wet areas, hinged wardrobes (easier to repair than sliding tracks) and lights and fans from mainstream brands that any electrician can service.</p>
<h3>4. Dust and traffic on Hosur Road</h3>
<p>Flats near Hosur Road and the elevated tollway collect fine dust. Closed storage is easier to live with than open shelving, and handle-less shutters with a groove collect less grime than profile handles. Traffic also matters during the project: a designer based across the city may visit once a week rather than twice.</p>
<h3>5. Society rules and handover clusters</h3>
<p>Large communities here set work hours, service-lift slots, a refundable deposit and sometimes a no-work-on-Sunday rule. When a project hands over hundreds of flats at once, good firms get booked for weeks. Start choosing a designer six to eight weeks before your keys arrive and read the <a href="/planning/new-home-handover-checklist/">new home handover checklist</a> before you sign off the builder's snag list.</p>

<h2>Kitchens that work in Electronic City flats</h2>
<p>The kitchen is where most of the budget and most of the daily wear go. For a typical 8×10 ft kitchen in this area, a sound standard-grade specification looks like this:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Carcass:</strong> BWP plywood for the sink unit and every base unit near water; BWR plywood or HDHMR for the rest. See the <a href="/materials/plywood-guide/">plywood guide</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Shutters:</strong> laminate on the base units for toughness; acrylic or a lighter laminate on the wall units if you want gloss.</li>
  <li><strong>Counter:</strong> black or dark granite, or quartz, sealed after fixing. Compare them in <a href="/compare/quartz-vs-granite/">quartz vs granite</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Storage:</strong> drawers rather than shelves under the hob, a corner solution in an L-shape, and a tall pantry unit if a wall is free.</li>
  <li><strong>Services:</strong> separate sockets for the chimney, mixer, microwave, purifier and refrigerator, fixed before tiling.</li>
</ul>
<?= img('l-shape-modular-kitchen-for-electronic-city-apartment', caption: 'An L-shape modular kitchen sized for an 8×10 ft Electronic City kitchen, with a tall unit and a loft') ?>
<p>A kitchen like this costs about ₹1.9–2.6 lakh in standard grade, including GST. The full price breakdown is in the <a href="/modular-kitchen/">modular kitchen guide</a>, and you can price your own layout in the <a href="/calculators/modular-kitchen/">kitchen calculator</a>. If you want it built locally, <?= huma('electronic_city', 'Huma Interiors builds modular kitchens for Electronic City homes') ?> in its own factory in Chandapura, about 10 km down Hosur Road.</p>

<h2>Living rooms in Electronic City apartments</h2>
<p>Most flats here have an L-shaped living and dining space with a balcony off the living room. The TV wall is usually the longest solid wall, opposite the sofa, and the dining table sits near the kitchen door. Three decisions shape the room:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The TV wall.</strong> A floating unit 7–8 ft wide with a panelled or fluted backdrop and concealed wiring. Keep the screen centre about 3.5 ft above the floor for a seated viewer.</li>
  <li><strong>The ceiling.</strong> A peripheral gypsum false ceiling with a cove light lifts the room and hides AC piping. Ceilings in these flats are often about 9.5–10 ft, so a 6–8 inch drop is enough.</li>
  <li><strong>The west-facing heat.</strong> Many towers here face west or south-west. Blackout or thermal curtains and a light wall colour keep the living room cooler in the afternoon.</li>
</ol>
<p>Ideas, sizes and costs for each of these are in our guide to <a href="/rooms/living-room-electronic-city/">living room interiors for Electronic City apartments</a>.</p>

<h2>How to choose an interior designer near Electronic City</h2>
<p>The method in our <a href="/planning/how-to-choose-interior-designer/">guide to choosing a designer</a> applies here too. Three local checks make it sharper:</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Distance to your site</strong>Ask where the office, the factory and the nearest live project are. A firm within 10–15 km, along Hosur Road, in Bommasandra or in Chandapura, can visit more often and send a carpenter quickly after handover.</li>
  <li><strong>A finished home in your type of building</strong>Ask to see a completed flat in a gated community like yours, ideally in Electronic City itself. Society rules, ceiling heights and kitchen sizes repeat within an area.</li>
  <li><strong>The factory</strong>Ask where the modules are made and visit if you can. Machine-cut panels and pressed edge banding last longer than site-made carcasses in a humid monsoon.</li>
  <li><strong>The quotation</strong>Insist on an itemised quote: board grade and brand for each cabinet group, shutter finish, hardware brand, countertop, sizes and GST on separate lines.</li>
  <li><strong>Payment and timeline</strong>Stage payments against milestones (design, production, installation, handover) and get the delivery date written into the contract.</li>
</ol>

